[{:seon.task/name "one-reference-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "Port v1's reference reader, `seon.ref` (read-only: ~/src/seon/src/seon/ref.clj; its tests ~/src/seon/test/seon/ref_test.clj; the rule in ~/src/seon/docs/seon/schedule/cards/TASK-CHAIN.md §3a), onto next/'s pillars. Keep from v1:
- one Markdown parse of any text: a plan, a doc page, a docstring;
- the delimiter picks the depth: a bare word that names a real entity is its summary; one backtick or a link is its doc; three or more backticks is its code; two backticks is a literal and never a reference;
- a name cited several times is shown once, at its strongest depth;
- a line citation (`src/seon/writer.clj:263`) is v1's shape: its candidate carries the file path, so the file is a reference, plus `:seon.ref/problem-kind :line-reference` (owner, 2026-10-21); whether that is red is `the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy`'s call;
- a section's summary is its heading plus its lead's first sentence (v1 `lead-sentence`, `section-view`).
Resolve names against next/'s program facts (`:var/name`), not v1's `:seon.fn/sym`. Drop v1's cache, sci and embedding hooks. Markdown on Jolt: the only Markdown library in awesome-jolt renders HTML and gives no tree, so write the minimum exact scanner for the CommonMark subset the rule needs (ATX headings with GitHub-style anchors, fenced code blocks of ``` or ~~~, code spans by CommonMark's equal-length backtick rule, inline links, paragraph boundaries for leads), proven by v1's ported tests and by CommonMark spec examples for that subset as table rows. One parse returns the value `docs-are-data-in-the-store` will store: sections (address, heading, lead, parent) and citations (what, depth, line).
Done by session REF (2026-10-21, see `docs/references.md`): the CommonMark subset proved sufficient against 70 spec examples, so the cmark fallback is not needed; one parse costs 0.8 ms and 1.2 MB on `AGENTS.md`, two thirds of it `seon.reader/read-text` per candidate name; `seon.context/current-state` also moved to `seon.ref/references`. Replaced `seon.reconcile/references` (backtick-only) everywhere: the context's current-state, `hairy` and the docs check. Read first: what v1 did here (above); there is no declared reference list or key anywhere (no `Describes` line, no hand-kept task reference keys, no `:pattern-of`); references are only what text cites."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.ref-test/one-text-gives-names-paths-and-line-citations-by-one-rule
                      seon.ref-test/the-delimiter-picks-the-depth
                      seon.ref-test/a-literal-and-fenced-code-are-not-references
                      seon.ref-test/a-bare-word-is-a-reference-only-when-it-names-something
                      seon.ref-test/a-name-is-shown-once-at-its-strongest-depth
                      seon.ref-test/a-line-citation-is-its-file-marked-line-reference
                      seon.ref-test/commonmark-examples-for-the-subset
                      seon.ref-test/a-section-summary-is-its-heading-and-leads-first-sentence
                      seon.ref-test/one-parse-costs-one-pass}}
 {:seon.task/name "docs-are-data-in-the-store"
  :seon.task/plan "The wiki (next/docs/*.md) is loaded into the store as data, written only where a page's digest changed, like task files (`seon.task/load-tx`): a section entity per heading with its address `topic#slug`, title, lead, own text and parent; its references are what its text cites, read by the one reader (`one-reference-reader`): links become refs to sections, cited names refs to var entities, file citations refs to file entities. A section's backlinks are who cites it, so a pattern's home knows its instances by its backlinks, with no extra key. A var's home is the section that cites it at code depth. Docs merge as data (a doc change on a branch merges like any other). Loaded at boot and on demand (`my.root/load-docs!`).
Done by session DOCS (2026-10-21, see `docs/references.md#the-wiki-in-the-store`): the entity is `:seon.section/fact` (resources/seon/schemas/seon.section.edn), written by `seon.docs/load-tx`, read by `seon.docs/section`, `seon.docs/backlinks` and `seon.docs/homes`. Differences from the plan above: the address is the parse's `path#anchor` (`docs/flow.md#idioms`), the form links resolve to, not `topic#slug`; no page digest is stored (root: no key beyond the section entity), so a reload compares each section's stated attributes with main's and an unchanged wiki answers [] at the cost of a parse and a pull per section (19 ms, 23 MB for docs/ today); a var's home is derived by re-reading its backlinks' text, so no depth is stored; there is no file entity, so file citations and schema keys are not stored (a file entity is root's call), and an unresolved name is not stored but left to `the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy` to report. Boot loading is `the-wiki-loads-at-boot`. A doc change on a branch merging as data was not proven here: the section's identity is `:seon.section/address`, so it merges as any identity-keyed entity does."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/one-reference-reader"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/an-unchanged-page-writes-nothing
                      seon.docs-test/a-changed-section-rewrites-only-itself
                      seon.docs-test/a-link-to-no-section-is-refused-by-name
                      seon.docs-test/backlinks-are-a-sections-instances}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-wiki-loads-at-boot"
  :seon.task/plan "A root's boot loads the wiki into main the way `my.root/load-docs!` does on demand (`seon.docs/load-tx` over `seon.docs/pages`), after the program facts so cited names resolve to their vars, and an unchanged wiki writes nothing. A refused load (a link to no section) is a problem fact on main naming the link, never a failed boot. Kept out of the first slice because session LOOP held `seon.root` (2026-10-21)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-data-in-the-store"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/a-booted-root-holds-the-wiki}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-cites-what-it-needs"
  :seon.task/plan "The context pulls the wiki sections a task's plan and instructions cite, at the depth each citation asks for (`docs/references.md`): a link or one backtick shows the section's doc view (its lead or opening, and the sections below), three backticks the section whole, a bare address its summary (heading and the lead's first sentence). Read from the sections main holds (`seon.docs/section`, loaded by `my.root/load-docs!`) and shown through `seon.ref/view`, one view per address at its strongest depth (`seon.ref/cited`), beside the names the context already resolves (`seon.context/current-state` `:references`). The task's names already reach it (`seon.task/a-task-cites-what-it-needs`, merged); this is the sections. Cut by root 2026-10-21 from the first plan: a done rule refusing a design with no citations (it judges where a proof should; v1 had none) and the `docs` search function (its own task when an agent needs it).
Done by session CTXDOCS (2026-10-21, see `docs/references.md#sections-in-a-tasks-context`): `seon.context/current-state` adds `:sections`, each cited path#anchor shown by `seon.docs/view` through `seon.ref/shown` (extracted from `seon.ref/view`). Differences from the plan above: a whole-page link and a bare prose address are not shown (main has no page entity; the reader keeps a bare word only when it names a file), and stored sections have no opening, so only a paragraph lead shows at doc depth."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-data-in-the-store"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-task-context-pulls-what-its-plan-cites-at-that-depth}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy"
  :seon.task/plan "(`seon.docs/homes` and `seon.docs/backlinks` were deleted unused, 2026-10-21; the code-depth home check rebuilds the query it needs: the sections whose `:seon.section/references` hold the var, filtered by the depth `seon.ref/parse` reads from their text.) `bin/check docs` is queries over the docs facts, run in every merged-result check: a reference that doesn't resolve is red; a section is stale when a var it cites at doc or code depth has a different defining form than at the page's last commit (read both with the reader, no text diff); health is reported first, then red: a var with two homes (two sections citing it at code depth), a section with no standalone lead sentence (v1's summary is heading + lead's first sentence), an orphan (no link and no map entry reaches it), a page linking nowhere, a dated changelog section. The style is written once, in docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept. A first set of these measures is jolt/patterns 2da2255 (`git show 2da2255`), built on Describes lines main ruled out; re-keyed on main's model it is: :two-homes (two sections citing one var at code depth), :no-lead, :lead-echoes-heading (the lead's words a subset of the heading's), :lead-too-long (over 40 words), :lead-cites (file:line in the lead), :heading-has-code (a code span or file:line in a heading), :orphan (no link or parent edge reaches it from docs/README.md; a page's sections are reached through the page), :isolated-page (links nowhere), :changelog (a dated heading, or two or more lines starting with a date; a date in prose is not one); README's groups are navigation and same-page anchors count as links. Its baseline on jolt/wiki's tree: two-homes 20, no-lead 32, lead-cites 18, lead-too-long 5, heading-has-code 14, orphan 13, isolated-page 3. Its three fixture deftests (a-var-with-two-homes-is-duplication-and-names-the-home, a-lead-that-is-missing-echoes-its-heading-runs-long-or-cites-is-flagged, a-heading-with-code-an-orphan-an-isolated-page-and-a-changelog-are-flagged) port with Describes lines replaced by code-depth citations."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-data-in-the-store"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/an-unresolved-reference-is-red
                      seon.docs-test/a-section-older-than-what-it-cites-is-stale
                      seon.docs-test/two-code-depth-homes-are-flagged
                      seon.docs-test/an-orphan-is-flagged}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-concept-has-its-page"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: docs/README.md is the map and states how the wiki is kept (patterns in their topic sections, linked; one home per concept, the section citing it at code depth; a standalone lead; everything linked; current truth). 16 pages. References are what text cites, read by `seon.ref/parse` (docs/references.md); there are no `Describes:` lines, no `:seon.task/docs` or `:seon.task/refs`, no `:seon/docs` metadata and no `:pattern-of` (task `seon.docs/one-reference-reader`). Root's procs, tasks, agents, checks, cost proofs, the server and merges have no page: they live in AGENTS.md, vocabulary rows and docstrings, so a task citing them pulls nothing.
What the branch did: jolt/wiki (8da07b4..11f34a1, session WIKI, 2026-10-21) wrote twelve concept pages (system, tasks, agents, check, context, cost, door, merge, printer, program, store, worktrees), docs/wiki.md (the section shape), patterns as `###` homes in system.md, Describes lines everywhere, `:seon.task/docs`/`:seon.task/refs` on 78 tasks, and a current-truth pass. Main took the rules (README) and rejected the declared keys.
What to apply, each section re-verified against main's code before it is written (the pages are a day older than main):
- New pages: [merge](docs/merge.md), [system](docs/system.md) (one section per proc in resources/seon/system.edn; its idioms stay homed in [flow](docs/flow.md#idioms-for-seon) and are linked), [tasks](docs/tasks.md) (task references stay in [references](docs/references.md#task-references)), [agents](docs/agents.md), [check](docs/check.md) (bin/check as it runs now; steps as tasks is `seon.reconcile/a-check-step-is-a-task`), [door](docs/door.md) (the server's one op table, held now in [parity](docs/parity.md#the-mcp-door); jolt/door's door.md is mostly untrue on main). [cost](docs/cost.md) (jolt/unitflake's re-targeted page, notes/worktrees/unitflake-retargeted-2026-10-22.diff, with jolt/wiki's cost.md folded in). Source text: `git show jolt/wiki:docs/<page>.md`.
- Into existing pages: store.md into [storage](docs/storage.md); printer.md and context.md into [render](docs/render.md); program.md into [source](docs/source.md); worktrees.md into [merging](docs/merging.md#a-session-works-in-a-worktree-of-its-own) and [machine](docs/machine.md); wiki.md's section shape (plain stable headings with no code, file:line or date; a lead of at most 40 words with no citation; the body cites vars, file:line only inside a function) into [README](docs/README.md#how-the-wiki-is-kept).
- Every page on the map, every new page linking its neighbours, each var's one home citing it at code depth; vocabulary.md links each word to its section.
- Current truth: session ids, t values, pids, dates and today/still/now become plain statements; nothing dated.
Not applied: every Describes line, `:one`/`:owner`/`:migrate`, `:seon.task/docs`, `:seon.task/refs`, `:seon/docs`, `:pattern-of`, `seon.docs/reached` (references are derived by the one parser). The task `seon.docs/the-wiki-stays-true-and-healthy` cites docs/wiki.md, which this task does not create: its plan is changed to cite README#how-the-wiki-is-kept.
Replaces: the knowledge held only in AGENTS.md and docstrings for these concepts (AGENTS.md links the pages rather than restating them); retires jolt/wiki, jolt/patterns-wiki-proof. Deletes no code."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.docs/docs-are-data-in-the-store"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.docs-test/the-wiki-loads-with-every-link-resolved
                      seon.docs-test/every-page-is-reached-from-the-map
                      seon.docs-test/every-section-leads-with-a-sentence-of-at-most-40-words}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo"
  :seon.task/plan "From jolt/patterns (35e1208; session ECOSYSTEM's idea from writ, notes/option3-ECOSYSTEM.md: a spec self-check rejects a law any implementation satisfies). The detector for \"a check that cannot fail\" (AGENTS.md section 9) and \"never a placebo a stub would pass\" (section 5), done mechanically: for each task's proofs, and each definition a proof reaches directly, run the proof against stubs of that definition: (constantly nil), identity, and its first argument unchanged. A proof that still passes against a stub does not test that definition: a placebo, recorded as a problem fact (class :proof, naming the proof, the definition and the stub that passed). The stub is a real definition in a forked test environment holding exactly the definitions the run wants (never with-redefs or alter-var-root), on the fork path of seon.agent/run-tests-forked. Cost: proofs x reached definitions x 3 stubs forked runs, bounded to the definitions the proof reaches directly. Seen while harvesting jolt/patterns: two of its idiom proofs exercised step-fns defined in the test file itself, which this would flag."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.program/definitions-from-the-compiler"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-proof-a-stub-passes-is-a-placebo seon.proofs-test/a-real-proof-fails-against-every-stub}}
